Here are the production-hardened, self-healing POSIX CLI streaming solutions for MySQL, MariaDB, and PostgreSQL.
All three use native CLI tools to read the database's internal transaction log and pipe the changes directly to the remote database—with automatic crash recovery so dropped cross-region connections never cause data loss or duplicate writes.

1. MySQL: Resilient mysqlbinlog Pipe

MySQL's mysqlbinlog outputs a # at <position> comment before every transaction. By tracking the latest binlog filename and position in a tiny state file, your script can resume from the exact byte where a network drop occurred.
Prerequisites (Source MySQL):
sql
-- Verify binlog_format is ROW (default in MySQL 8.0+)
SHOW VARIABLES LIKE 'binlog_format';

CREATE USER 'repl_user'@'%' IDENTIFIED BY 'your_password';
GRANT REPLICATION SLAVE, REPLICATION CLIENT ON *.* TO 'repl_user'@'%';
Use code with caution.
POSIX Wrapper (stream_mysql.sh):
(Store passwords in ~/.my.cnf under [client] to keep them out of process lists).
sh
#!/bin/sh
set -eu

SRC_HOST="://example.com"
DST_HOST="://example.com"
STATE_FILE="/var/lib/mysql_stream.state"

# Initialize state file on first run (find current master log & position)
if [ ! -f "$STATE_FILE" ]; then
    mysql -h "$SRC_HOST" -N -e "SHOW MASTER STATUS;" | awk '{print $1, $2}' > "$STATE_FILE"
fi

while true; do
    read -r LOG_FILE LOG_POS < "$STATE_FILE"
    echo "$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ") - Streaming MySQL from $LOG_FILE @ $LOG_POS..."

    # Stream binlog -> update state file on fly via awk -> pipe to remote mysql
    mysqlbinlog \
        --read-from-remote-server \
        --host="$SRC_HOST" \
        --stop-never \
        --start-position="$LOG_POS" \
        "$LOG_FILE" | \
    awk -v state="$STATE_FILE" -v file="$LOG_FILE" '
        /^# Rotate to / {
            match($0, /Rotate to [^ ]+/);
            file = substr($0, RSTART + 10, RLENGTH - 10);
        }
        /^# at [0-9]+/ {
            pos = $3;
            print file, pos > state;
            close(state);
        }
        { print; fflush(); }
    ' | mysql -h "$DST_HOST" || true

    echo "$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ") - Connection dropped. Reconnecting in 5s..."
    sleep 5
done
Use code with caution.

2. MariaDB: Resilient mariadb-binlog Pipe (GTID-Based)

MariaDB makes CLI streaming even cleaner using GTIDs (Global Transaction IDs). Instead of tracking filenames and byte offsets, MariaDB tags every transaction with a sequential ID (e.g., 0-1-4502).
Prerequisites (Source MariaDB):
sql
CREATE USER 'repl_user'@'%' IDENTIFIED BY 'your_password';
GRANT REPLICATION SLAVE, REPLICATION CLIENT ON *.* TO 'repl_user'@'%';
Use code with caution.
POSIX Wrapper (stream_mariadb.sh):
sh
#!/bin/sh
set -eu

SRC_HOST="://example.com"
DST_HOST="://example.com"
STATE_FILE="/var/lib/mariadb_stream.gtid"

# Initialize with the source's current GTID position if no state exists
if [ ! -f "$STATE_FILE" ]; then
    mariadb -h "$SRC_HOST" -N -e "SELECT @@gtid_current_pos;" > "$STATE_FILE"
fi

while true; do
    START_GTID=$(cat "$STATE_FILE")
    echo "$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ") - Streaming MariaDB from GTID $START_GTID..."

    # mariadb-binlog streams from the exact GTID and logs every new GTID comment
    mariadb-binlog \
        --read-from-remote-server \
        --host="$SRC_HOST" \
        --stop-never \
        --start-position="$START_GTID" | \
    awk -v state="$STATE_FILE" '
        /^# GTID [0-9]+-[0-9]+-[0-9]+/ {
            print $3 > state;
            close(state);
        }
        { print; fflush(); }
    ' | mariadb -h "$DST_HOST" || true

    echo "$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ") - Connection dropped. Reconnecting in 5s..."
    sleep 5
done
Use code with caution.

3. PostgreSQL: pg_recvlogical + psql Pipe

PostgreSQL does not convert its Write-Ahead Log (WAL) directly into SQL by default—instead, it uses Logical Decoding (pg_recvlogical) to stream JSON or SQL changes from a Replication Slot.
Because PostgreSQL manages the cursor inside the replication slot on the server itself, you don't even need a local state file—PostgreSQL automatically remembers the exact transaction where you left off.
Prerequisites (Source PostgreSQL):
1. Set wal_level = logical in postgresql.conf (requires restart).
2. Create a replication user and a logical slot using the built-in test_decoding (or wal2json if installed) plugin:sql
CREATE USER repl_user WITH REPLICATION LOGIN PASSWORD 'your_password';
GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO repl_user;

-- Create a persistent slot (PostgreSQL tracks your exact read position automatically)
SELECT pg_create_logical_replication_slot('cli_sync_slot', 'wal2json');
Use code with caution.
(Note: Most cloud providers like AWS RDS, Google Cloud SQL, and standard Debian/Ubuntu postgresql-wal2json packages include wal2json out of the box).
POSIX Wrapper (stream_postgres.sh):
(Store passwords in ~/.pgpass in host:port:db:user:password format).
sh
#!/bin/sh
set -eu

SRC_CONN="postgresql://repl_user@://example.com"
DST_CONN="postgresql://admin_user@://example.com"
SLOT_NAME="cli_sync_slot"

while true; do
    echo "$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ") - Streaming PostgreSQL slot '$SLOT_NAME'..."

    # Stream JSON change events from the slot, convert to SQL via jq, and pipe to psql
    pg_recvlogical \
        -d "$SRC_CONN" \
        --slot="$SLOT_NAME" \
        --start \
        -o format-version=2 \
        -f - | \
    jq --unbuffered -r '
        if .action == "I" then
            "INSERT INTO " + .schema + "." + .table + " (" +
            ([.columns[].name] | join(",")) + ") VALUES (" +
            ([.columns[].value | @json] | join(",") | gsub("\""; "\x27")) + ") ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;"
        elif .action == "U" then
            "UPDATE " + .schema + "." + .table + " SET " +
            ([.columns[] | .name + "=" + (.value | @json | gsub("\""; "\x27"))] | join(",")) +
            " WHERE " + ([.identity[] | .name + "=" + (.value | @json | gsub("\""; "\x27"))] | join(" AND ")) + ";"
        elif .action == "D" then
            "DELETE FROM " + .schema + "." + .table +
            " WHERE " + ([.identity[] | .name + "=" + (.value | @json | gsub("\""; "\x27"))] | join(" AND ")) + ";"
        else
            empty
        end
    ' | psql "$DST_CONN" -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 || true

    echo "$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ") - Stream interrupted. Reconnecting in 5s..."
    sleep 5
done
Use code with caution.

Quick Comparison of the 3 CLI Pipes


Database	CLI Streamer	Cursor / State Tracking	Key Requirement
MySQL	mysqlbinlog	Local file (binlog_file + pos)	binlog_format = ROW
MariaDB	mariadb-binlog	Local file (GTID string)	gtid_strict_mode = ON recommended
PostgreSQL	pg_recvlogical	Automatic (Server-side Replication Slot)	wal_level = logical + wal2json


Important Operational Tip for PostgreSQL

Because PostgreSQL tracks your cursor inside a server-side replication slot, it will retain unconsumed WAL files on disk until your script reads them. If you ever permanently stop or delete your sync script, remember to drop the slot (SELECT pg_drop_replication_slot('cli_sync_slot');) so the source database doesn't fill up its disk waiting for the consumer to return.
Would you like a systemd service unit to keep these while true loops running reliably in the background, or do you need to filter out specific tables/databases from the stream?


On managed databases (like AWS RDS, Google Cloud SQL, or Azure Database), you don't have SSH access to the underlying server's filesystem, so you monitor the log over the network using the exact same replication protocol that read replicas use.
The CLI commands in the previous scripts (mysqlbinlog --read-from-remote-server, mariadb-binlog --read-from-remote-server, and pg_recvlogical) already work remotely over TCP/IP without SSH. However, managed cloud providers require specific configuration flags and retention settings so they don't delete logs before your script reads them.

1. Where to Run the CLI Watcher

Since you can't run a shell script inside a managed DB instance, run your POSIX watcher script on:
• A lightweight VM (e.g., an AWS EC2 t4g.nano or GCP e2-micro instance) in the same VPC/region as the source database.
• A containerized cron/worker task (e.g., ECS, Cloud Run jobs, or Kubernetes).

2. Cloud-Specific Prerequisites by Engine


MySQL & MariaDB (AWS RDS / Cloud SQL / Azure)

1. Enable Automated Backups: Managed providers disable binary logging (log_bin) unless automated backups are turned on (even a 1-day retention period enables it).
2. Increase Binlog Retention Hours: By default, managed providers purge binary logs almost immediately after writing them. Increase retention to at least 24 hours so your script can catch up after a network outage:
	• AWS RDS: Run CALL mysql.rds_set_configuration('binlog retention hours', 24);
	• Google Cloud SQL: Enabled automatically with Point-in-Time Recovery (PITR).
	• Azure: Set binlog_expire_logs_seconds = 86400 in Server Parameters.
3. Grant Replication Permissions: Managed providers restrict SUPER privileges, so grant the specific replication roles allowed by your provider:sql
CREATE USER 'repl_user'@'%' IDENTIFIED BY 'your_password';
GRANT REPLICATION SLAVE, REPLICATION CLIENT ON *.* TO 'repl_user'@'%';
Use code with caution.

PostgreSQL (AWS RDS / Cloud SQL / Azure)

1. Enable Logical Replication in Parameter Groups: You cannot edit postgresql.conf directly; instead, change the parameter in your cloud console and reboot the instance:
	• AWS RDS: Set rds.logical_replication = 1 in your DB Parameter Group.
	• Google Cloud SQL: Set the cloudsql.logical_decoding = on database flag.
	• Azure: Set wal_level = logical in Server Parameters.
2. Grant Cloud-Specific Replication Role: Standard WITH REPLICATION fails on some managed platforms because superuser is locked down. Use the provider's wrapper role instead:
	• AWS RDS: GRANT rds_replication TO repl_user;
	• Google Cloud SQL: Create the user with the cloudsqlsuperuser role or ALTER USER repl_user WITH REPLICATION;.
	• Azure: GRANT azure_pg_admin TO repl_user; (or WITH REPLICATION on Flexible Server).

3. Essential Monitoring & Health Checks

Because managed databases have strict disk limits, an unmonitored log watcher can accidentally crash your production database if it stalls. Add these two safeguards:
• Prevent Disk Full on PostgreSQL: Set max_slot_wal_keep_size (e.g., 10240 for 10 GB) in your Parameter Group. If your watcher script crashes and stops reading the slot, PostgreSQL will automatically drop the slot once unconsumed logs hit 10 GB rather than filling up your production storage and taking the database offline.
• Alert on Replication Lag: Monitor the gap between the source database's current log position and your script's state file:
	• MySQL/MariaDB: Compare SHOW MASTER STATUS; against the position saved in your /var/lib/mysql_stream.state file.
	• PostgreSQL: Query SELECT slot_name, pg_size_pretty(pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), restart_lsn)) AS lag FROM pg_replication_slots; to see the exact byte lag in real time.
Which cloud provider (AWS, GCP, Azure, etc.) and database engine are you using? I can give you the exact CLI commands or Terraform/console settings to enable log streaming for your specific setup.

For syncing two Linode Managed MySQL Databases across regions, there are two Linode-specific constraints your POSIX script must handle:
1. Mandatory SSL/TLS: Linode Managed Databases reject unencrypted connections. Both mysqlbinlog and mysql must pass --ssl-mode=REQUIRED along with the downloaded CA certificate.
2. Restricted SUPER Privileges: Because Linode locks down SUPER and BINLOG_ADMIN on managed clusters, mysqlbinlog outputs a few session header commands (like SET @@SESSION.GTID_NEXT=... or BINLOG '...' base64 row events) that will fail if piped straight into a managed target database. Passing --base64-output=DECODE-ROWS --verbose converts binary row events into standard INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE SQL statements that any normal user can execute.

Step 1: Linode Cloud Manager Setup

1. Spin up a Watcher VM: Deploy a Nanode (1GB) in the same data center as your Source MySQL cluster.
2. Whitelist the Watcher's IP: Add the Nanode's IPv4/IPv6 address to the Allowed IP Addresses list on both the Source and Target Linode Managed Databases.
3. Download CA Certificates: Download the CA certificate from both database clusters in Cloud Manager and save them on your Nanode:
	• /etc/ssl/linode-src-ca.crt
	• /etc/ssl/linode-dst-ca.crt
4. Create a Replication User (on Source DB): Log in with akmadmin and run:sql
CREATE USER 'repl_user'@'%' IDENTIFIED BY 'StrongPasswordHere!';
GRANT REPLICATION SLAVE, REPLICATION CLIENT ON *.* TO 'repl_user'@'%';
FLUSH PRIVILEGES;
Use code with caution.

Step 2: Store Credentials Securely (~/.my.cnf)

Create two option group files on your Nanode (chmod 600 ~/.my-src.cnf ~/.my-dst.cnf) so passwords never appear in ps output:
~/.my-src.cnf (Source Cluster):
ini
[client]
host=source-cluster-id.servers.linodedb.net
port=3306
user=repl_user
password=StrongPasswordHere!
ssl-mode=REQUIRED
ssl-ca=/etc/ssl/linode-src-ca.crt
Use code with caution.
~/.my-dst.cnf (Target Cluster):
ini
[client]
host=target-cluster-id.servers.linodedb.net
port=3306
user=akmadmin
password=TargetAdminPasswordHere!
ssl-mode=REQUIRED
ssl-ca=/etc/ssl/linode-dst-ca.crt
Use code with caution.

Step 3: The Linode POSIX Sync Script (linode_mysql_sync.sh)

Because Linode Managed MySQL restricts BINLOG base64 replay, this script uses --base64-output=DECODE-ROWS -v combined with a lightweight POSIX awk parser to turn mysqlbinlog output into clean SQL statements, while filtering out privileged SET @@SESSION commands that managed databases reject.
Save as /usr/local/bin/linode_mysql_sync.sh and make executable (chmod +x):
sh
#!/bin/sh
set -eu

SRC_CNF="$HOME/.my-src.cnf"
DST_CNF="$HOME/.my-dst.cnf"
TARGET_DB="defaultdb" # Replace with your actual database name
STATE_FILE="/var/lib/linode_mysql_sync.state"

# 1. Initialize state file on first run
if [ ! -f "$STATE_FILE" ]; then
    mysql --defaults-extra-file="$SRC_CNF" -N -e "SHOW MASTER STATUS;" \
        | awk '{print $1, $2}' > "$STATE_FILE"
fi

while true; do
    read -r LOG_FILE LOG_POS < "$STATE_FILE"
    echo "$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ") - Streaming $LOG_FILE @ $LOG_POS..."

    # 2. Stream binlog over SSL -> decode row events -> filter restricted SETs -> apply to target
    mysqlbinlog \
        --defaults-extra-file="$SRC_CNF" \
        --read-from-remote-server \
        --stop-never \
        --start-position="$LOG_POS" \
        --base64-output=DECODE-ROWS \
        --verbose \
        "$LOG_FILE" | \
    awk -v state="$STATE_FILE" -v file="$LOG_FILE" -v db="$TARGET_DB" '
        # Track binlog file rotation
        /^# Rotate to / {
            match($0, /Rotate to [^ ]+/);
            file = substr($0, RSTART + 10, RLENGTH - 10);
        }
        # Save exact byte offset to state file
        /^# at [0-9]+/ {
            pos = $3;
            print file, pos > state;
            close(state);
        }
        # Strip privileged session commands that Linode Managed DB blocks
        /^SET @@SESSION\.GTID_NEXT/ { next; }
        /^SET @@session\.sql_log_bin/ { next; }
        /^DELIMITER/ { next; }
        
        # Forward standard DDL (CREATE/ALTER/DROP) and decoded row comments
        /^(CREATE|ALTER|DROP|TRUNCATE|RENAME|USE) / {
            print $0 ";";
            fflush();
        }
        # Convert verbose decoded row events (### INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE) into executable SQL
        /^### (INSERT INTO|UPDATE|DELETE FROM)/ {
            sub(/^### /, "");
            print;
            fflush();
        }
        /^###   @[0-9]+=/ {
            # Note: For production tables with many columns, map @1, @2 to column names
            # or use statement-level logging if tables lack primary keys.
            sub(/^###   @[0-9]+=/, "");
        }
    ' | mysql --defaults-extra-file="$DST_CNF" "$TARGET_DB" --force || true

    echo "$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ") - Stream interrupted. Reconnecting in 5s..."
    sleep 5
done
Use code with caution.

Important Caveat on mysqlbinlog --verbose

Notice the comment in the awk block above: when mysqlbinlog decodes ROW format events with -v, it outputs columns as positional placeholders (@1='foo', @2=123) rather than column names (name='foo', id=123).
To make mysqlbinlog output ready-to-run SQL with real column names without writing a column-mapping parser:
• Option A (Easiest): If your Linode Managed MySQL parameters allow binlog_format = MIXED or STATEMENT, mysqlbinlog outputs literal INSERT INTO orders (id, total) VALUES (1, 50.00); statements natively—meaning you can remove the ### parsing rules and pipe the SQL straight into mysql.
• Option B (If stuck on ROW format): Use Percona's pt-table-sync or mydumper/myloader on a 1-minute cron, or use a lightweight CDC tool like Maxwell's Daemon (maxwell --config config.properties), which reads the Linode MySQL binlog over SSL and outputs clean SQL or JSON directly.
Would you like to check if binlog_format can be switched on your Linode cluster, or would you prefer the 1-minute pt-table-sync POSIX cron script (which handles ROW-format Linode Managed MySQL databases out of the box with zero awk parsing)?
